<body>

<div class="header">
  <h1>BIEK Computer Science 12th Class Past Paper Analysis</h1>
  <p>A comprehensive trend analysis of question types, frequency patterns, and topic distribution across 11 years of HSC Part-2 examination papers (2015&ndash;2025)</p>
  <div class="meta">Board of Intermediate Education Karachi &bull; 327 Questions Analyzed &bull; Report generated April 2026</div>
</div>

<div class="container">

  <!-- KPI Strip -->
  <div class="kpi-strip">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">327</div>
      <div class="label">Total Questions</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">10</div>
      <div class="label">Exam Years Covered</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">32.7</div>
      <div class="label">Avg Questions / Year</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="value">15 / 14.7 / 3</div>
      <div class="label">Avg MCQ / Short / Long</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Overview -->
  <div class="section" id="overview">
    <h2>1. Dataset &amp; Examination Overview</h2>
    <p>This report examines <strong>327 examination questions</strong> extracted from BIEK Computer Science HSC Part-2 papers spanning 2015 to 2025. The dataset captures three question formats&mdash;Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs), Short Questions, and Long Questions&mdash;across <strong>10 examination years</strong> present in the data.</p>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Data note:</strong> The year <strong>2020</strong> is absent from the dataset, likely due to COVID-19 pandemic disruptions to examinations. All analyses account for this gap; no data has been fabricated for 2020.
    </div>

    <h3>Overall Question Type Distribution</h3>
    <div class="chart-row">
      <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="overallPie"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Question Type</th><th>Count</th><th>Share</th><th>Avg / Year</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><span class="badge badge-blue">MCQs</span></td><td>150</td><td>45.9%</td><td>15.0</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="badge badge-green">Short</span></td><td>147</td><td>45.0%</td><td>14.7</td></tr>
            <tr><td><span class="badge badge-yellow">Long</span></td><td>30</td><td>9.2%</td><td>3.0</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight"><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>327</strong></td><td><strong>100%</strong></td><td><strong>32.7</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="insight-box">
          <strong>Key finding:</strong> MCQs and Short Questions each account for approximately 45&ndash;46% of the exam, while Long Questions consistently make up about 9%. This near-parity between MCQ and Short question volume is a defining characteristic of the BIEK CS exam format.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 2: Year-by-Year Volume -->
  <div class="section" id="yearly-distribution">
    <h2>2. Year-by-Year Question Volume Analysis</h2>
    <p>The total number of questions per exam has fluctuated within a narrow band of 27 to 36, with a notable structural shift identifiable in the data.</p>

    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:900px;">
      <canvas id="yearlyBar" height="300"></canvas>
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Year</th><th>MCQs</th><th>Short</th><th>Long</th><th>Total</th><th>YoY Change</th><th>% Change</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2015</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td><td>27</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2016</td><td>15</td><td>9</td><td>3</td><td>27</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr class="highlight"><td>2017</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>36</td><td>+9</td><td>+33.3%</td></tr>
        <tr class="highlight"><td>2018</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>36</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr class="highlight"><td>2019</td><td>15</td><td>18</td><td>3</td><td>36</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td style="color:var(--red);">2020</td><td colspan="6" style="color:var(--text-light); font-style:italic;">No data available (likely COVID-19 disruption)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2021</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td>&minus;3*</td><td>&minus;8.3%*</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2022</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2023</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2024</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2025</td><td>15</td><td>15</td><td>3</td><td>33</td><td>0</td><td>0.0%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="font-size:0.8rem; color:var(--text-light);">* YoY change for 2021 is computed relative to 2019 due to the absence of 2020 data.</p>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Three distinct structural eras are visible:</strong><br>
      <strong>Era 1 (2015&ndash;2016):</strong> Compact format with 27 questions (9 Short).<br>
      <strong>Era 2 (2017&ndash;2019):</strong> Expanded format with 36 questions (18 Short)&mdash;a 33.3% increase driven entirely by doubling Short Questions.<br>
      <strong>Era 3 (2021&ndash;2025):</strong> Settled at 33 questions (15 Short), a moderate reduction from the peak but still above the original baseline.
    </div>

    <h3>Year-over-Year Total Questions Trend</h3>
    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:800px;">
      <canvas id="yoyLine" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 3: Question Type Composition -->
  <div class="section" id="question-type-analysis">
    <h2>3. Question Type Composition &amp; Trends</h2>
    <p>While the overall exam structure appears stable on the surface, the proportion of question types has shifted meaningfully due to changes in Short Question volume.</p>

    <h3>Percentage Composition by Year</h3>
    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:900px;">
      <canvas id="stackedBar" height="300"></canvas>
    </div>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Year</th><th>MCQ %</th><th>Short %</th><th>Long %</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>2015</td><td>55.6%</td><td>33.3%</td><td>11.1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2016</td><td>55.6%</td><td>33.3%</td><td>11.1%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2017</td><td>41.7%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>8.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2018</td><td>41.7%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>8.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2019</td><td>41.7%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>8.3%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2021&ndash;2025</td><td>45.5%</td><td>45.5%</td><td>9.1%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="chart-row">
      <div>
        <h3>MCQ Count: Constant at 15</h3>
        <p>MCQ count has remained <strong>perfectly stable at 15 questions</strong> across all 10 years. This is the most rigid structural element of the exam. However, as the total question count has changed around it, its <em>proportional share</em> has shifted from 55.6% (2015&ndash;16) down to 41.7% (2017&ndash;19) and now sits at 45.5% (2021&ndash;25).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Long Questions: Constant at 3</h3>
        <p>Long Questions have also been <strong>constant at 3 per exam</strong> across all years. Each Long Question typically contains 2&ndash;3 sub-parts (separated by &ldquo;$&rdquo; delimiters), covering a mix of programming and database topics. Their proportional share has ranged from 8.3% to 11.1%.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>The Short Question is the sole variable element.</strong> It jumped from 9 (2015&ndash;16) to 18 (2017&ndash;19), then settled at 15 (2021&ndash;25). MCQs and Long Questions have never changed. This means any year-over-year volume fluctuation is <em>entirely</em> attributable to Short Question count. Educators should focus preparation effort on Short Questions, as this is where the exam has the most flexibility and scope variation.
    </div>

    <h3>Short Question Volume Over Time</h3>
    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:700px;">
      <canvas id="shortLine" height="260"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 4: Topic Patterns -->
  <div class="section" id="topic-patterns">
    <h2>4. Recurring Topics &amp; Question Content Patterns</h2>
    <p>A keyword-based content analysis of all 327 questions reveals the dominant conceptual areas tested by BIEK. Each question was categorized by matching against a curated set of Computer Science topic keywords. A single question may match multiple topics.</p>

    <h3>Top Topics by Frequency (All Years Combined)</h3>
    <div id="topicBars">
      <!-- Generated by JS -->
    </div>

    <h3>Topic Distribution Across Question Types</h3>
    <p>Different question types emphasize different topic areas. MCQs lean toward factual recall (data types, DB objects), while Long Questions favor deeper conceptual treatment (control structures, database models, functions).</p>

    <div class="chart-container" style="max-width:900px;">
      <canvas id="topicByType" height="380"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Topic Frequency Heatmap by Year</h3>
    <p>The following heatmap shows how topic coverage has shifted across years. Darker cells indicate higher frequency.</p>

    <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
    <table class="heatmap-table" id="heatmapTable">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th style="text-align:left;">Topic</th>
          <th>2015</th><th>2016</th><th>2017</th><th>2018</th><th>2019</th>
          <th>2021</th><th>2022</th><th>2023</th><th>2024</th><th>2025</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody id="heatmapBody">
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Perennial top-3 topics:</strong> Data Types, Database/DBMS, and DB Objects appear in every single year&rsquo;s top themes. <strong>Operators</strong> and <strong>I/O Functions</strong> form a reliable secondary tier. Topics like <strong>normalization, error types, and flowcharts</strong> appear sporadically&mdash;these represent lower-probability but still testable areas.
    </div>

    <h3>Emerging and Declining Topics</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div>
        <h3 style="color: #38a169;">Increasing Presence (2021&ndash;2025)</h3>
        <ul style="padding-left:1.2rem; font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li><strong>Array/Structure:</strong> Appeared minimally in early years, but present in 2022&ndash;2025 with questions on pointers, structures, and unions.</li>
          <li><strong>Normalization &amp; ACID:</strong> First explicit mention of ACID properties in 2025; normalization appears in 2022 and 2024.</li>
          <li><strong>Advanced DB keys:</strong> Super keys, candidate keys, and alternate keys appear from 2024&ndash;2025, expanding beyond just primary/foreign key questions.</li>
          <li><strong>Bitwise operators &amp; ternary operator:</strong> First appear in 2025, reflecting a broadening of operator coverage.</li>
        </ul>
